# Normalise line endings in the repository, always.
#
# Why this file exists: the repo already stores every file with LF endings
# (verified: 0 of 64 blobs contain CRLF, and `git add --renormalize .` produces
# no changes). But with no .gitattributes, whether a *new* file gets stored as
# LF or CRLF depends on whatever core.autocrlf happens to be on the machine that
# committed it. This box has core.autocrlf=true, so it was warning on every
# single file: "LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it".
#
# The rule below makes the behaviour a property of the repository rather than of
# anyone's git config, so:
#   - text files are stored LF in the repo (diffs stay line-ending agnostic)
#   - they are checked out as-is on Linux/macOS, and as CRLF on Windows where
#     that is what the tools expect
#   - the warnings stop, because the outcome is no longer in doubt
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-native scripts: these genuinely need CRLF, or cmd.exe misparses them.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Lock files are generated. Committing them is correct, but never let a tool
# rewrite their line endings -- a reformatted lock file is a real diff that
# hides whatever actually changed.
package-lock.json -diff
client/package-lock.json -diff
server/package-lock.json -diff

# Generated by client/test-audio.mjs on every run (it transpiles audio.ts into a
# stub so the audio path can be tested outside a browser). Ignored in
# .gitignore; repeated here so the reason is recorded next to the rule.
client/test-audio.stub.mjs -diff linguist-generated=true

# Language stats: the arena is the game, the tests are the specification.
*.ts    text
*.mjs   text
*.cjs   text
*.json  text
*.md    text
*.html  text
*.css   text
